      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64332,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 295,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Microglandular hyperplasia is usually an incidental finding, or it may present with contact bleeding.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:01:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64333,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 295,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "<p>Microglandular hyperplasia is typically identified in women of reproductive age.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-08T18:08:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64334,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 295,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This lesion is often associated with pregnancy and progestogen therapy.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:01:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64335,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 295,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:01:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64336,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 295,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Microglandular hyperplasia may be multifocal. Larger lesions may be polypoid and ulcerated.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:01:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64337,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 295,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There are clusters of crowded small glands lined by cuboidal to columnar mucinous epithelium. Subnuclear vacuolation and reserve cell hyperplasia are often present. The nuclei are uniform, with absent or scant mitoses. The stroma may be inflamed and hyalinized. Rare cases demonstrate solid or trabecular growth patterns, cytological atypia, signet-ring&ndash;like appearances, or increased mitotic activity {2535775; 25083971}. Immunohistochemistry is unnecessary for diagnosis, although the glandular epithelium is usually positive for ER, PAX2, and cyclin&nbsp;D1 and negative for p16, vimentin, and CEA, and it shows a Ki-67 proliferation index of &lt;&nbsp;10% {25473758; 12383213; 12819393}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:01:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64338,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 295,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cytological changes are nonspecific but can lead to a misdiagnosis of adenocarcinoma or high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion {7924805}. There is often a spectrum of clearly benign to 3D clusters of atypical glandular cells. Nuclei are frequently enlarged, hyperchromatic, and overlapping, but they have smooth nuclear contours and fine chromatin. Repair and degenerative changes (including pseudoparakeratosis) may also be present.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:01:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64339,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 295,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:01:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
